8.6 Strong Induction and Well-Ordering
Strong Induction
Strong induction is similar to regular induction, but the assumption is stronger.

Instead of assuming only:

"The statement is true for n"

you assume:

"The statement is true for all values from 1 through n"

and then prove it for n + 1.

When Strong Induction Is Useful
Use strong induction when solving a problem requires knowledge of several previous cases instead of just one.

Examples include:

Fibonacci numbers
Recursive algorithms
Factoring integers
Tree structures in computer science
Well-Ordering Principle
The Well-Ordering Principle states:

Every non-empty set of positive integers contains a smallest element.

This principle is closely related to induction and can be used to prove many of the same mathematical statements.

Relationship to Fibonacci
Strong induction is often used with the Fibonacci Sequence because each new term depends on previous terms, not just a single earlier value.

Example:

F(5) depends on F(4) and F(3)
F(10) depends on F(9) and F(8)
This makes Fibonacci one of the classic examples for strong induction.

Key Takeaway
Strong induction assumes all previous cases are true, making it useful for proving statements involving recursion and sequences such as Fibonacci numbers.
